# qa.fayth — the QA persona: sweeps the scar record, proposes test beads for unguarded
# defects, and records what it looked at whether or not it finds anything.
#
# A fayth is a persona DEFINITION; an aeon is one summoned instance of it. Four things:
# a predicate carving its partition out of the graph, the memories it must read, the tools
# it may use, and what wakes it.

FAYTH_NAME=qa
FAYTH_DESC="QA — sweeps the scar record and proposes test beads for unguarded defects"

# The fence. The fence check in aeon.sh requires FAYTH_LABELS to contain SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL
# (when non-empty) before both sweep and claim modes; it runs before the sweep branch and
# cannot be skipped. QA runs in beadless sweep mode (aeon.sh --sweep) on every timer tick —
# it does not claim qa-sweep beads. The 'qa-sweep' suffix is kept so that a manually-filed
# sweep bead is still claimable if one is ever created by hand.
FAYTH_LABELS="${SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL:+$SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL,}qa-sweep"
FAYTH_EXCLUDE_LABELS="spira-poison,$SPIRA_ASK_LABEL,$SPIRA_CI_LABEL"

# Statutes only. QA is recognition and enumeration: it proposes, it does not build, and a
# shelf of runbooks for production incidents would push the statutes it actually needs off
# the context budget.
FAYTH_MEMORY_PREFIXES="law-"

# NO FAYTH_REPO. The repository comes from the BEAD — a `repo:<name>` label resolved
# through repo-map. The sweep bead carries no repo: label, so aeon.sh falls back to
# spira_home_repo(), which is the harness's own home checkout.

# HAIKU. QA is recognition and enumeration rather than construction — it reads a small,
# well-defined scar record, classifies each entry, and proposes a bead. This is exactly the
# shape the small model handles well: pattern-matching over a structured set, not creative
# synthesis. It is also the persona that runs most often after Ops, so its model is the
# throughput dial the operator turns when the five-hour window fills with confirmations
# rather than findings.
#
# VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED. `claude --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 -p ...` answers on this
# box. The dated id is the one that answers — the catalog at CLI 2.1.263 rejects
# claude-haiku-5 and claude-haiku-4-6 outright. An id the CLI rejects does not fail loudly:
# every summon dies and the role looks merely quiet, which is the shape of outage this
# harness is worst at noticing. Probe a candidate id before writing it here.
FAYTH_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT=1

# THE QA LANE — QA must never compete with builders for the aeon pool. A sweep that is
# waiting on a pool slot already occupied by builders misses exactly the defects that
# escaped during a busy sprint. FAYTH_LANE=qa places it in a named partition whose capacity
# is its own FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT, never drawn from SPIRA_MAX_AEONS.
#
# The qa lane is declared in SPIRA_LANES (default: "ops qa"), and a user who wants
# QA-level scheduling for their own persona names their lane there and sets FAYTH_LANE=qa
# in their fayth file — without editing this one.
FAYTH_LANE=qa

# BOUNDED BY THE SWEEP INTERVAL. The timer (spira-qa.timer) fires every six hours. A QA
# session that outlives its cadence accumulates a backlog of unread snapshots, and since
# QA is recognition over a small set — not a long build — eight minutes of reading is
# already a generous budget. The wall enforces the exit before the next sweep arrives.
#
# FAYTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS is the hard wall: systemd kills the unit at this number. The lease
# and heartbeat are set to survive one heartbeat gap plus the claim; a session killed at the
# wall must expire its lease promptly so the next sweep can start clean.
#
# ONE HOUR, HARD. The scar record for `scars` depth is small (closed bug beads + incidents);
# `modules` adds a ranking query; `wide` adds a structural pass. One hour is the ceiling
# that keeps six-hour sweeps from overlapping. The lease matches so a killed session's bead
# is reclaimable before the next sweep fires.
FAYTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600
FAYTH_LEASE_MINUTES=70
FAYTH_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=120
FAYTH_STALL_BEATS=8

# READ-ONLY TOOLSET. QA reads the scar record, queries the database, and files beads. It
# does not write code; it does not edit tests. Those are builder tasks the bead it files
# will carry. Withholding Edit and Write is the mechanism that enforces "QA proposes; it
# does not land tests itself" — the judgement that produced the 17-minute gate.
FAYTH_TOOLS="Bash,Read,Glob,Grep,TodoWrite"
